    total: 0-4 across all pipe rows [observed]
  single_exit_constraint: |
    After gate opening (each gate -> 3-tall vertical 6-post at center, flanks become bg),
    every pipe segment (contiguous bg between 6-posts/holes/edges) must have
    <=1 exit below and <=1 entry above. For 2 gates in same wall row with
    centers c1<c2 and c2-c1>2: a separator (6-post from adjacent wall's gate
    or hole) must exist between c1+1 and c2-1 in both pipe rows above and below.
  transformation: |
    1. Rotate each gate: 3 cells->bg, center col gets vertical 6-post (rows r-1,r,r+1).
    2. Remove tokens, simulate falling: each token drops through single-exit
       segments until stuck (no exit). Drops span 0-6 levels; max per example >=3.
    3. Pack tokens at opposite end of segment from entry point. Tokens from same
       side stack inward (1-2 tokens per destination segment)./

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    - Pick H=2*N+1 (N in 5-9) and W in 14-20.
    - Build the opened-gate pipe network (the output state) top-down. For each
      wall row, place 0-3 gate-post columns (vertical 6-posts). Ensure the
      single-exit constraint is satisfied by construction -- when placing a new
      gate-post, verify each resulting segment in pipe rows above and below has
      <=1 exit/entry, adding holes if needed.
    - Simulate token settling in the output network to place output tokens.
      Each pipe row (except last) contributes 2 tokens (left+right edge); route
      them downward and pack at segment endpoints. Ensure at least one token
      traverses >=3 levels for non-trivial paths.
    - Invert to get input -- undo gate rotation (replace each 3-tall 6-post
      with 1x3 horizontal 7-bar in the wall row, remove 6-posts from pipe rows),
      move tokens back to edge positions (col 0 and col W-1 of their starting
      pipe rows). Holes (0 in pipe rows) remain unchanged.
